      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "    \nTo find \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\)\n   - \\(VW = 3\\)\n\n2. **Determine the length of \\(WX\\):**\n   In a right triangle, the Pythagorean theorem states:\n   \\[\n   VX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying both sides:\n   \\[\n   13 = 9 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(WX^2\\):\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 13 - 9 = 4\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides:\n   \\[\n   WX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the sides of the triangle:**\n   - \\(VW = 3\\)\n   - \\(XV = \\sqrt{13}\\)\n   - \\(WX = 2\\)\n\n4. **Find \\(\\tan V\\):**\n   The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side.\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{VW}{XV}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Rationalize the denominator:**\n   To rationalize the denominator, multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{13}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}}\n\\]",
