      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\), we first need to determine the length of the side \\(WX\\). We know that \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\) and \\(VW = 3\\). Since \\(\\angle VWX\\) is a right angle, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of \\(WX\\).\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Here, \\(VX\\) is the hypotenuse, and \\(VW\\) and \\(WX\\) are the other two sides. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\nVX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation, we have:\n\n\\[\n13 = 9 + WX^2\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 9 from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nWX^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\nWX = 2\n\\]\n\nNow that we know \\(WX = 2\\), we can find \\(\\tan V\\). In a right triangle, the tangent of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. Here, \\(\\tan V\\) is the ratio of \\(WX\\) to \\(VW\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{WX}{VW} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
