      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in triangle \\(VWX\\), we start by identifying the sides of the triangle and using the definitions of the trigonometric functions.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\)\n- \\(VW = 3\\)\n- \\(WX = b\\) (we need to find this first)\n\nSince \\(VW\\) and \\(VX\\) are sides of a right triangle with \\(WX\\) as the hypotenuse, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(WX\\):\n\\[\nVX^2 + VW^2 = WX^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{13})^2 + 3^2 = b^2\n\\]\n\\[\n13 + 9 = b^2\n\\]\n\\[\nb^2 = 22\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\sqrt{22}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\tan V\\). The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. For \\(\\angle V\\), the opposite side is \\(WX = \\sqrt{22}\\) and the adjacent side is \\(VW = 3\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{3}}\n\\]",
